[Line "4.Bg5 a6 5.Qd2 b5 6.a4 b4 7.Nd5 a5 8.c3 c6 9.Ne3 Nf6"]
[Section "4.Bg5 and 4.Bc4"]
[Summary "Against 4.Bg5 Black avoids the Pirc ...Nf6 and plays the Tiger set-up. Without a bishop on e3 the ...c5 break is easier, and the knight on d5 can be chased away by ...c6."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Bg5 { Aims at the pin on f6 and at e7, and prepares Qd2 and long castling. Against a Pirc order (4...Nf6) it is more dangerous, because White may play e5 or Qd2 with threats
against the knight (in the elite games White scores 53% against 4...Nf6 and 46% against 4...a6). } 4... a6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gb8d7] The Tiger answer: no knight to pin, and Black waits for White to commit. Against the bishop on g5, Black's counterplay with ...c5 is
easier than against Be3, because the bishop no longer protects d4. } 5. Qd2 { [%cal Gd2h6,Gg5h6] Threatens Bh6, but without a knight on f6 and a castled king the threat has little point. } 5... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4] } 6. a4 { Challenges the pawn at once. }
( 6. Nf3 Bb7 7. Bd3 Nd7 { Black develops as in the Tiger lines: ...Ngf6 and ...c5 follow. } )
6... b4 { [%cal Gb4c3] Gains time on the knight. } 7. Nd5 { [%cal Gd5c7,Gd5e7] The knight is well placed in the centre. } 7... a5 { Protects the b4 pawn and stops a5, which would leave Black weak on the queenside. }
8. c3 { Attacks b4 and prepares to open the queenside. } 8... c6 { [%cal Gc6d5] Kicks the knight. } 9. Ne3 { The knight has to retreat; e3 is the natural square, from where it covers c2 and f5 and keeps an eye on d5. } 9... Nf6! { [%cal Gf6e4]
Hits e4 and forces f3, which blocks the knight on g1. } 10. f3 O-O 11. cxb4 axb4 12. Qxb4 Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Ge7e5] White has a pawn, but Black has a lead in development and a clear plan: ...h6 forces the bishop to decide, and ...e5 and ...Nc5 give Black the centre. }
13. Qd2 h6 14. Bh4 e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] } 15. d5 Nc5 { [%cal Gc5e4,Gc5b3] } 16. Rd1 g5 17. Bf2 Ncxe4 { A forcing sequence from the study: 18.fxe4 Nxe4 19.Qb4 Nxf2 20.Kxf2 c5 and the white king is exposed, Black has the bishops and
a big centre. It is a model of what Black is playing for here, rather than a line to expect move for move: Black has real compensation for the pawn. } *

[Line "4.Bc4 Nf6 5.Nf3 O-O 6.O-O Nc6 7.h3: the Mad Dog"]
[Section "4.Bg5 and 4.Bc4"]
[Summary "White aims at f7 with the bishop on c4. Black develops with ...Nf6, ...O-O and ...Nc6; after the slow 7.h3 the knight takes on e4 and ...d5 forks bishop and knight."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Bc4 { The bishop aims at f7. White hopes for an attack with Qe2 or Qf3 and a knight to g5, but with ...Nf6 Black has e5 under control and can answer almost everything with ...d5. } 4... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4]
The knight comes out at once: it prevents e5 and attacks the e4-pawn. } 5. Nf3 O-O { Castling removes the king from the f7 problem: f7 is now defended by the rook and the king. } 6. O-O Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gc8g4]
The Mad Dog. The knight puts d4 under pressure with the bishop on g7 behind it. White must now deal with the possible ...Bg4 pin and ...Nxe4. }
( 6... Bg4 { An alternative that scores very well in the elite games (63% for Black after 7.h3). } 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nc6 )
7. h3?! { Looks natural, since it prevents ...Bg4, but it neglects e4. White scores badly after this move in the elite database. } ( 7. e5 dxe5 8. dxe5 Qxd1 9. Rxd1 { The e5-pawn is attacked by the knight and the bishop, and Black has easy equality. } )
7... Nxe4 { [%cal Ge4c3,Ge4f2] } 8. Nxe4 ( 8. Bxf7+ Rxf7 9. Nxe4 d5 10. Nc5 Qd6 11. Be3 e5 12. dxe5 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. c3 c6 { White has given up a bishop for a pawn
and Black's pieces are more active. } ) 8... d5! { [%cal Gd5c4,Gd5e4] The pawn forks the bishop on c4 and the knight on e4. } 9. Bd3 ( 9. Bxd5 Qxd5 { White has lost a piece for a pawn. } ) 9... dxe4 10. Bxe4 { [%cal Rc6d4] } 10... Nxd4 { [%cal Gd4f3,Gd8d4] Three pieces attack d4 and only two defend it. The knight is protected. }
11. Nxd4 { Black wins a pawn: after 11...Bxd4 the queen can't take on d4 because ...Qxd4 would win it, and the bishop on e4 is loose. The study judges the position as slightly better for Black. } *

[Line "4.Bc4 Nf6 5.Qe2 c6! 6.e5 dxe5 7.dxe5 Nd5 8.Bd2"]
[Section "4.Bg5 and 4.Bc4"]
[Summary "White prepares e5 with Qe2. Black answers with ...c6, which takes d5 and b5 away from White and meets e5 with a trade on d5."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Bc4 Nf6 5. Qe2 { Defends e4 and prepares e5. In the elite database it is uncomfortable for Black after 5...O-O (6.Nf3 and 6.e5 both score above 54% for White) and after 5...Nc6
(6.e5 scores 67%). } 5... c6! { [%cal Gc6d5,Gd8a5] The best-scoring reply in the elite database (47% for Black against 36% after 5...O-O and 32% after 5...Nc6). The pawn takes d5 and b5 away from White's pieces, meets e5 with ...dxe5 and ...Nd5, and
prepares ...b5 and ...Qa5. Black castles later, when the bishop on c4 and the queen on e2 are no longer aimed at his kingside. } 6. e5 { The critical test. } 6... dxe5 7. dxe5 Nd5 { [%cal Gd5c3,Gd5b4] The knight
attacks c3 and takes the sting out of the e5-pawn. } 8. Bd2 { Black has easy development: ...O-O, ...Nb6 or ...Nxc3 and ...Qa5 are all possible, and the e5-pawn is weak. In 29 elite games White scored 34% and Black 52% here. } *
